/*  purelaws01 -- methanol, 2-propanol and water in one liquid (DEV.md 4c C398,
    slice (b), the witness of addendum A1).

    WHAT IT SHOWS.  The liquid viscosity block below names NO model.  Each
    constituent then takes the law its own record marks preferred -- methanol
    and water their `vogel` law (both records hold `andrade` and `vogel`, and
    say `preferred vogel;`), 2-propanol its only law, the five-constant form
    `chemsepEq101` -- and the mixing rule combines the three pure values.
    Before slice (b) a model word picked the SAME sub-block in every record,
    so this mixture could not be priced at all: `model Vogel;` refuses
    2-propanol (no `vogel` law) and `model chemsepEq101;` refuses methanol and
    water.  The run's resolved view prints, per constituent, which law of
    which record it took, who chose it and the law's window.

    THE MIXING RULE.  None is written, so the property's default answers
    (Grunberg-Nissan, C331), and with no interaction row declared every pair
    is treated as G_ij = 0, which the rule announces: the mixture is the
    log-mole average of the pure values.  No measured mixture viscosity is
    claimed here; the case shows where each pure number comes from.

    Activity model ideal: nothing here flashes.  Not sealed: the records come
    from data/standards/components.  */

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components ( methanol isopropanol water );

equilibrium
{
    formulation gammaPhi;
    liquid { activityModel { model ideal; } }
    vapour { fugacityModel idealGas; }
}

transport
{
    liquid
    {
        viscosity { }      // no word: each record's preferred law, mixed by the default rule
    }
}
